1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,648
Collectors gave results shape. Now expand elements inside the pipeline.

2
00:00:04,648 --> 00:00:08,379
One input can become many outputs — without nested loops.

3
00:00:08,379 --> 00:00:12,045
flatMap is map plus flatten — the one-to-many transform.

4
00:00:12,045 --> 00:00:15,646
Composition chains small steps into readable pipelines.

5
00:00:15,646 --> 00:00:19,377
Today — flatten complexity instead of hiding it in loops.

6
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:22,978
Streams express structure. flatMap expresses expansion.

7
00:00:22,978 --> 00:00:24,814
Episode Twenty-Eight.

8
00:00:24,814 --> 00:00:29,012
flatMap and composition — one-to-many pipelines.

9
00:00:29,012 --> 00:00:32,021
map transforms one element into one result.

10
00:00:32,021 --> 00:00:35,940
flatMap transforms one element into a stream of results.

11
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:39,369
The stream is flattened into the parent pipeline.

12
00:00:39,369 --> 00:00:42,518
List of lists becomes a single flat sequence.

13
00:00:42,518 --> 00:00:46,997
Optional values become present elements — absent ones drop away.

14
00:00:46,997 --> 00:00:50,286
Think expand, then merge — not nested for-each.

15
00:00:50,286 --> 00:00:53,517
Composition means chaining focused operations.

16
00:00:53,517 --> 00:00:57,380
Each step does one job — filter, map, flatMap, collect.

17
00:00:57,380 --> 00:01:00,541
Read pipelines top to bottom like a sentence.

18
00:01:00,541 --> 00:01:03,983
Extract a method when a chain grows hard to name.

19
00:01:03,983 --> 00:01:07,355
Good composition favors clarity over cleverness.

20
00:01:07,355 --> 00:01:10,024
Small steps compose into big behavior.

21
00:01:10,024 --> 00:01:13,788
Without flatMap, nested structures invite nested loops.

22
00:01:13,788 --> 00:01:17,277
Orders with line items. Departments with employees.

23
00:01:17,277 --> 00:01:21,041
A map gives you a stream of collections — still nested.

24
00:01:21,041 --> 00:01:25,010
flatMap unwraps each inner collection into the outer flow.

25
00:01:25,010 --> 00:01:27,542
One pipeline replaces index juggling.

26
00:01:27,542 --> 00:01:31,442
Flatten at the right level — not too early, not too late.

27
00:01:31,442 --> 00:01:34,896
One-to-many shows up everywhere in real domains.

28
00:01:34,896 --> 00:01:38,206
Split a sentence into words. Parse CSV fields.

29
00:01:38,206 --> 00:01:41,515
Expand a user into their roles or permissions.

30
00:01:41,515 --> 00:01:45,904
flatMap with Arrays.stream or Collection.stream is idiomatic.

31
00:01:45,904 --> 00:01:49,934
Choose flatMap when the natural result is many, not one.

32
00:01:49,934 --> 00:01:52,596
If you only need one, map is simpler.

33
00:01:52,596 --> 00:01:54,984
Common patterns worth memorizing.

34
00:01:54,984 --> 00:01:59,036
flatMap(Optional::stream) drops empty optionals cleanly.

35
00:01:59,036 --> 00:02:03,087
flatMap(Collection::stream) flattens nested collections.

36
00:02:03,087 --> 00:02:06,488
flatMap(s -> s.lines()) splits text into lines.

37
00:02:06,488 --> 00:02:10,033
distinct and sorted still apply after flattening.

38
00:02:10,033 --> 00:02:14,230
Compose terminal collectors at the end — shape stays last.

39
00:02:14,230 --> 00:02:15,677
Three common mistakes.

40
00:02:15,677 --> 00:02:20,151
One — using map when flatMap is required — you get Stream of Stream.

41
00:02:20,151 --> 00:02:23,900
Two — flattening too eagerly and losing grouping context.

42
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:27,782
Three — giant flatMap lambdas that should be named methods.

43
00:02:27,782 --> 00:02:31,992
Also — forgetting that order is preserved in sequential streams.

44
00:02:31,992 --> 00:02:34,952
Readable steps beat one opaque flatMap block.

45
00:02:34,952 --> 00:02:37,747
Interview question — map versus flatMap?

46
00:02:37,747 --> 00:02:41,241
map — one input, one output element in the stream.

47
00:02:41,241 --> 00:02:44,944
flatMap — one input, zero or more outputs, flattened.

48
00:02:44,944 --> 00:02:48,787
Give examples — words from a line, items from an order.

49
00:02:48,787 --> 00:02:52,490
Mention Optional::stream for filtering absent values.

50
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:55,914
That answer shows you understand stream geometry.

51
00:02:55,914 --> 00:03:00,161
Pipelines flatten nicely. Next — parallelism with care.

52
00:03:00,161 --> 00:03:03,173
Episode Twenty-Nine — Parallel Streams.

53
00:03:03,173 --> 00:03:06,339
When fork-join helps — and when it hurts.

54
00:03:06,339 --> 00:03:07,420
See you there.
